{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"code","value":"function injectRateLimiter<TFn, TSelected>(\n   fn,\n   options,\nselector?): AngularRateLimiter<TFn, TSelected>;","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Defined in: "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/pacer/blob/main/packages/angular-pacer/src/rate-limiter/injectRateLimiter.ts#L121","children":[{"type":"text","value":"rate-limiter/injectRateLimiter.ts:121"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An Angular function that creates and manages a RateLimiter instance."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is a lower-level function that provides direct access to the RateLimiter's functionality.\nThis allows you to integrate it with any state management solution you prefer."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rate limiting is a simple \"hard limit\" approach that allows executions until a maximum count is reached within\na time window, then blocks all subsequent calls until the window resets. Unlike throttling or debouncing,\nit does not attempt to space out or collapse executions intelligently."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The rate limiter supports two types of windows:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"'fixed': A strict window that resets after the window period. All executions within the window count\ntowards the limit, and the window resets completely after the period."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"'sliding': A rolling window that allows executions as old ones expire. This provides a more\nconsistent rate of execution over time."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For smoother execution patterns:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use throttling when you want consistent spacing between executions (e.g. UI updates)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use debouncing when you want to collapse rapid-fire events (e.g. search input)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use rate limiting only when you need to enforce hard limits (e.g. API rate limits)"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"state-management-and-selector","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State Management and Selector"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The function uses TanStack Store for state management and wraps it with Angular signals.\nThe "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"selector"},{"type":"text","value":" parameter allows you to specify which state changes will trigger signal updates,\noptimizing performance by preventing unnecessary updates when irrelevant state changes occur."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, there will be no reactive state subscriptions"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and you must opt-in to state\ntracking by providing a selector function. This prevents unnecessary updates and gives you\nfull control over when your component tracks state changes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Available state properties:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"executionCount"},{"type":"text","value":": Number of function executions that have been completed"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"executionTimes"},{"type":"text","value":": Array of timestamps when executions occurred for rate limiting calculations"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rejectionCount"},{"type":"text","value":": Number of function executions that have been rejected due to rate limiting"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"cleanup-on-destroy","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cleanup on Destroy"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUnmount"},{"type":"text","value":" option to run a callback when the component is destroyed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-parameters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Parameters"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"tfn","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TFn"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFn"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"extends"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AnyFunction"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"tselected","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TSelected"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TSelected"},{"type":"text","value":" = {\n}"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"parameters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Parameters"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"fn","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fn"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFn"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../type-aliases/AngularPacerOptions.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AngularPacerOptions"}]},{"type":"text","value":"<"},{"type":"link","href":"../interfaces/AngularRateLimiterOptions.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AngularRateLimiterOptions"}]},{"type":"text","value":"<"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFn"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TSelected"},{"type":"text","value":">>"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"selector","children":[{"type":"text","value":"selector?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":") => "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TSelected"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"returns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Returns"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../interfaces/AngularRateLimiter.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AngularRateLimiter"}]},{"type":"text","value":"<"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFn"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TSelected"},{"type":"text","value":">"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"example","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Default behavior - no reactive state subscriptions\nconst rateLimiter = injectRateLimiter(apiCall, {\n  limit: 5,\n  window: 60000,\n  windowType: 'sliding',\n});\n\n// Opt-in to track execution count changes\nconst rateLimiter = injectRateLimiter(\n  apiCall,\n  {\n    limit: 5,\n    window: 60000,\n    windowType: 'sliding',\n  },\n  (state) => ({ executionCount: state.executionCount })\n);\n\n// Monitor rate limit status\nconst handleClick = () => {\n  const remaining = rateLimiter.getRemainingInWindow();\n  if (remaining > 0) {\n    rateLimiter.maybeExecute(data);\n  } else {\n    showRateLimitWarning();\n  }\n};\n\n// Access the selected state (will be empty object {} unless selector provided)\nconst { executionCount, rejectionCount } = rateLimiter.state();","lang":"ts"}],"headings":[{"id":"state-management-and-selector","text":"State Management and Selector","level":2},{"id":"cleanup-on-destroy","text":"Cleanup on Destroy","level":2},{"id":"type-parameters","text":"Type Parameters","level":2},{"id":"tfn","text":"TFn","level":3},{"id":"tselected","text":"TSelected","level":3},{"id":"parameters","text":"Parameters","level":2},{"id":"fn","text":"fn","level":3},{"id":"options","text":"options","level":3},{"id":"selector","text":"selector?","level":3},{"id":"returns","text":"Returns","level":2},{"id":"example","text":"Example","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n```ts\nfunction injectRateLimiter<TFn, TSelected>(\n   fn,\n   options,\nselector?): AngularRateLimiter<TFn, TSelected>;\n```\n\nDefined in: [rate-limiter/injectRateLimiter.ts:121](https://github.com/TanStack/pacer/blob/main/packages/angular-pacer/src/rate-limiter/injectRateLimiter.ts#L121)\n\nAn Angular function that creates and manages a RateLimiter instance.\n\nThis is a lower-level function that provides direct access to the RateLimiter's functionality.\nThis allows you to integrate it with any state management solution you prefer.\n\nRate limiting is a simple \"hard limit\" approach that allows executions until a maximum count is reached within\na time window, then blocks all subsequent calls until the window resets. Unlike throttling or debouncing,\nit does not attempt to space out or collapse executions intelligently.\n\nThe rate limiter supports two types of windows:\n- 'fixed': A strict window that resets after the window period. All executions within the window count\n  towards the limit, and the window resets completely after the period.\n- 'sliding': A rolling window that allows executions as old ones expire. This provides a more\n  consistent rate of execution over time.\n\nFor smoother execution patterns:\n- Use throttling when you want consistent spacing between executions (e.g. UI updates)\n- Use debouncing when you want to collapse rapid-fire events (e.g. search input)\n- Use rate limiting only when you need to enforce hard limits (e.g. API rate limits)\n\n## State Management and Selector\n\nThe function uses TanStack Store for state management and wraps it with Angular signals.\nThe `selector` parameter allows you to specify which state changes will trigger signal updates,\noptimizing performance by preventing unnecessary updates when irrelevant state changes occur.\n\n**By default, there will be no reactive state subscriptions** and you must opt-in to state\ntracking by providing a selector function. This prevents unnecessary updates and gives you\nfull control over when your component tracks state changes.\n\nAvailable state properties:\n- `executionCount`: Number of function executions that have been completed\n- `executionTimes`: Array of timestamps when executions occurred for rate limiting calculations\n- `rejectionCount`: Number of function executions that have been rejected due to rate limiting\n\n## Cleanup on Destroy\n\nUse the `onUnmount` option to run a callback when the component is destroyed.\n\n## Type Parameters\n\n### TFn\n\n`TFn` *extends* `AnyFunction`\n\n### TSelected\n\n`TSelected` = \\{\n\\}\n\n## Parameters\n\n### fn\n\n`TFn`\n\n### options\n\n[`AngularPacerOptions`](../type-aliases/AngularPacerOptions.md)\\<[`AngularRateLimiterOptions`](../interfaces/AngularRateLimiterOptions.md)\\<`TFn`, `TSelected`\\>\\>\n\n### selector?\n\n(`state`) => `TSelected`\n\n## Returns\n\n[`AngularRateLimiter`](../interfaces/AngularRateLimiter.md)\\<`TFn`, `TSelected`\\>\n\n## Example\n\n```ts\n// Default behavior - no reactive state subscriptions\nconst rateLimiter = injectRateLimiter(apiCall, {\n  limit: 5,\n  window: 60000,\n  windowType: 'sliding',\n});\n\n// Opt-in to track execution count changes\nconst rateLimiter = injectRateLimiter(\n  apiCall,\n  {\n    limit: 5,\n    window: 60000,\n    windowType: 'sliding',\n  },\n  (state) => ({ executionCount: state.executionCount })\n);\n\n// Monitor rate limit status\nconst handleClick = () => {\n  const remaining = rateLimiter.getRemainingInWindow();\n  if (remaining > 0) {\n    rateLimiter.maybeExecute(data);\n  } else {\n    showRateLimitWarning();\n  }\n};\n\n// Access the selected state (will be empty object {} unless selector provided)\nconst { executionCount, rejectionCount } = rateLimiter.state();\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: injectRateLimiter\ntitle: injectRateLimiter\n---\n\n```ts\nfunction injectRateLimiter<TFn, TSelected>(\n   fn,\n   options,\nselector?): AngularRateLimiter<TFn, TSelected>;\n```\n\nDefined in: [rate-limiter/injectRateLimiter.ts:121](https://github.com/TanStack/pacer/blob/main/packages/angular-pacer/src/rate-limiter/injectRateLimiter.ts#L121)\n\nAn Angular function that creates and manages a RateLimiter instance.\n\nThis is a lower-level function that provides direct access to the RateLimiter's functionality.\nThis allows you to integrate it with any state management solution you prefer.\n\nRate limiting is a simple \"hard limit\" approach that allows executions until a maximum count is reached within\na time window, then blocks all subsequent calls until the window resets. Unlike throttling or debouncing,\nit does not attempt to space out or collapse executions intelligently.\n\nThe rate limiter supports two types of windows:\n- 'fixed': A strict window that resets after the window period. All executions within the window count\n  towards the limit, and the window resets completely after the period.\n- 'sliding': A rolling window that allows executions as old ones expire. This provides a more\n  consistent rate of execution over time.\n\nFor smoother execution patterns:\n- Use throttling when you want consistent spacing between executions (e.g. UI updates)\n- Use debouncing when you want to collapse rapid-fire events (e.g. search input)\n- Use rate limiting only when you need to enforce hard limits (e.g. API rate limits)\n\n## State Management and Selector\n\nThe function uses TanStack Store for state management and wraps it with Angular signals.\nThe `selector` parameter allows you to specify which state changes will trigger signal updates,\noptimizing performance by preventing unnecessary updates when irrelevant state changes occur.\n\n**By default, there will be no reactive state subscriptions** and you must opt-in to state\ntracking by providing a selector function. This prevents unnecessary updates and gives you\nfull control over when your component tracks state changes.\n\nAvailable state properties:\n- `executionCount`: Number of function executions that have been completed\n- `executionTimes`: Array of timestamps when executions occurred for rate limiting calculations\n- `rejectionCount`: Number of function executions that have been rejected due to rate limiting\n\n## Cleanup on Destroy\n\nUse the `onUnmount` option to run a callback when the component is destroyed.\n\n## Type Parameters\n\n### TFn\n\n`TFn` *extends* `AnyFunction`\n\n### TSelected\n\n`TSelected` = \\{\n\\}\n\n## Parameters\n\n### fn\n\n`TFn`\n\n### options\n\n[`AngularPacerOptions`](../type-aliases/AngularPacerOptions.md)\\<[`AngularRateLimiterOptions`](../interfaces/AngularRateLimiterOptions.md)\\<`TFn`, `TSelected`\\>\\>\n\n### selector?\n\n(`state`) => `TSelected`\n\n## Returns\n\n[`AngularRateLimiter`](../interfaces/AngularRateLimiter.md)\\<`TFn`, `TSelected`\\>\n\n## Example\n\n```ts\n// Default behavior - no reactive state subscriptions\nconst rateLimiter = injectRateLimiter(apiCall, {\n  limit: 5,\n  window: 60000,\n  windowType: 'sliding',\n});\n\n// Opt-in to track execution count changes\nconst rateLimiter = injectRateLimiter(\n  apiCall,\n  {\n    limit: 5,\n    window: 60000,\n    windowType: 'sliding',\n  },\n  (state) => ({ executionCount: state.executionCount })\n);\n\n// Monitor rate limit status\nconst handleClick = () => {\n  const remaining = rateLimiter.getRemainingInWindow();\n  if (remaining > 0) {\n    rateLimiter.maybeExecute(data);\n  } else {\n    showRateLimitWarning();\n  }\n};\n\n// Access the selected state (will be empty object {} unless selector provided)\nconst { executionCount, rejectionCount } = rateLimiter.state();\n```\n"}
